1) Observing the program

When connecting/running, you see a menu:

    (0) Workshop Absence
    (1) Test Absence
    (2) Member Insult
    (3) Redeem Portal Access Key (cost 1000)
    (4) Revoke a Confession
    (5) Exit

You start with 100 points, but the key requires 1000, so normal play won’t reach it.
2) Reverse engineering (Ghidra)

Search for the string:

text

"How many do you want to confess?"

Ghidra shows three similar confession handlers:

    FUN_0040162a (item 0)
    FUN_004016ce (item 1)
    FUN_00401772 (item 2)

Compare them:
Safe handlers (0 and 1)

Both include a negative check:

C code :

if (iVar1 < 0) {
  puts("Invalid amount!");
}

So negative quantities are rejected there.
Vulnerable handler (Member Insult)

FUN_00401772 does not check iVar1 < 0. It only checks if quantity is larger than stock:

C code :

if (DAT_00404150 < iVar1) puts("Not enough available!");

So if you enter a negative number, e.g. -10:

    stock < -10 is false → passes
    it reaches the “affordability” check and then applies the transaction

This function also calls:

C code : 

FUN_004015a0(2, DAT_0040414c, iVar1);

The 2 matches menu option (2) Member Insult

3) Why negative increases points

The apply/update logic effectively behaves like:

C code :

points = points - (rate * quantity);

If quantity is negative:

C code :

points = points - (rate * -10)
points = points + (rate * 10)

So you gain points.

4) Exploitation steps

    Choose (2) Member Insult
    Enter a negative quantity (example: -50)
    Repeat until points ≥ 1000
    Choose (3) Redeem Portal Access Key
    The program prints the flag (remote reads it from flag.txt)

Example manual interaction:

text

Choose an option: 2
How many do you want to confess? -50
Confession recorded!

... repeat until >= 1000 ...

